A||||3||buds||sub box dimensions?||||5||
Z||000000||buds||01-26-2001||01:08 PM||||any car audio experts know the proper cu.in required for 2 8" subs(orion xtr 4 ohms) I am building a system for my project #2 car (corvette) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||01-26-2001||02:41 PM||||Orion XTR's in 8" require .4 Cubic Feet of room. Depending on specifc application, You can go down to .33 cuft for a tighter bass or up to .5cuft if you went with a ported box. Basically a 12" X 12" X 6"  box per speaker using 1/2" thick MFD wood.<BR>Outside dimensions.<BR>12 X 12 X 6 = .4cuft<BR>12 X 10 X 6 = .34cuft (tighter bass)<BR>12 X 12 X 7 = .51cuft for ported<P>This is Per Speaker.<BR>So you can make a box 24 X 12 X 6 and have both speakers in it.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||buds||01-26-2001||08:20 PM||||thanks for the reply Oreif, another question.<BR>the box i've drawn up is 36" wide x 14" deep x 7" high, how do I figure out the cu.ft.? also with this box size am I better to go with 2 10". Am I better to divide the box in half so each sub has its own space? If I was to port the box, what size ports or does that matter? I quess I had a few more questions than I thought. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||01-27-2001||12:36 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by buds:<BR><B>thanks for the reply Oreif, another question.<BR>the box i've drawn up is 36" wide x 14" deep x 7" high, how do I figure out the cu.ft.? also with this box size am I better to go with 2 10". Am I better to divide the box in half so each sub has its own space? If I was to port the box, what size ports or does that matter? I quess I had a few more questions than I thought.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The box you list is 2.04 cu.ft. Just take each number of inches and divide by 12<BR>36 / 12 = 3<BR>14 / 12 = 1.16<BR>7 / 12 = .58<BR>Then .58 X 1.16 = .672<BR>then .672 X 3 = 2.0<BR>Now you need to subtract the volume the speakers take up. Usually known as VAS.<BR>So the box is 2.0 cu.ft but once a Pair of 10" woofers are in it your probably down to about 1.8 cu.ft. or .9 cu ft per speaker. Which is OK for a sealed box with 10's. Now as for seperating the speakers with an internal wall. If you are going to drive both speakers with a mono signal you don't need a wall. It is usually better to not put in a wall. The only time you need a wall is if you were to put in stereo channels (left and right) because each speaker would move differently. The best way to go is no wall and mono channels to the speakers.<BR>If you wanted ports you would need to get to about 2.2cu.ft. The port for an average pair of 10's would be 3" diameter and 4.5" long.<BR>(Each 10 would need 1.5" diameter.) It's location would need to be between the 2 speakers. If you are into Jazz/dance/rap or running low power (under 100W per speaker) you can go ported. If you listen to rock/classical or high power (over 100W per speaker) go with a sealed box. Sealed boxes have a "sharp" pound and ported boxes are a little more "Boom" sound. Ported boxes also require lower power levels to reach lower frequencies but distort quickly at higher power levels. ||||reg||1
